    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/sima-qian"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sima-qian.jpg" alt="Sima Qian" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/sima-qian">Sima Qian</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Si_maqian.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuezhi">Yuezhi</a> first appear in Chinese sources as a powerful pastoral people of the north-western frontier. According to the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/records-of-the-grand-historian">Shiji</a></em> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sima-qian">Sima Qian</a>, which draws on the report of the envoy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhang-qian">Zhang Qian</a>, they originally lived between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunhuang">Dunhuang</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qilian-mountains">Qilian Mountains</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu-corridor">Gansu corridor</a>, and in the late third century BCE they were strong enough to look down on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a>. Their origins are obscure. Many scholars identify them with speakers of the Tocharian languages, an early branch of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-european-languages">Indo-European</a> later attested in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarim-basin">Tarim Basin</a>, and link them to the Tochari whom Greek writers name among the conquerors of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a>; others are cautious, since no Yuezhi text survives. Some also connect them with the 'Yuzhi' named as suppliers of jade in early Chinese texts, which would make them middlemen in the jade trade from Khotan, but this too is uncertain. Archaeologists have sought their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantustan">homeland</a> among the cultures of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu">Gansu</a> corridor and the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tian-shan">Tianshan</a> without reaching consensus. What is clear is that by about 300 BCE a Yuezhi confederation controlled the pastures and routes of the corridor and dominated its neighbours. The Xiongnu ruler Touman even sent his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modu-chanyu">Modu</a> to them as a hostage, a sign of Yuezhi superiority at the time.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/khalchayan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/khalchayan.jpg" alt="Khalchayan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/khalchayan">Khalchayan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kushan_prince_portrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ALFGRN, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuezhi">Yuezhi</a> were mounted herders who, in the words of the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/records-of-the-grand-historian">Shiji</a></em>, moved about with their animals and shared the customs of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a>. Horses, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> and sheep were their wealth, and the mountain pastures of the Qilian range and the oases of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu-corridor">Gansu corridor</a> allowed seasonal movement between highland and lowland grazing. Their position on the corridor made them intermediaries between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and the lands to the west, through which horses, jade and Chinese goods passed. After their migration to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> they ruled the settled, urbanised population of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a>, with its irrigated farmland, Greek-founded cities and markets. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhang-qian">Zhang Qian</a> described Daxia, as the Chinese called Bactria, as a land of walled towns and skilled merchants without a strong ruler, and noted that the Yuezhi lived north of the Gui, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxus">Amu Darya</a>, and treated Daxia as a subject people. The Yuezhi elite gradually adopted the urban culture of their subjects. Their early coinage imitated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greco-bactrian-kingdom">Greco-Bactrian</a> issues, and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalchayan">Khalchayan</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a> a palace or hall was decorated with painted clay sculptures of nomad aristocrats in Hellenistic style. Rich graves at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tillya-tepe">Tillya Tepe</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, with some 20,000 gold objects, probably belonged to nomad nobles of the same period, whether Yuezhi or Saka.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/zhang-qian"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zhang-qian.jpg" alt="Zhang Qian" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/zhang-qian">Zhang Qian</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ZhangQianTravels.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PHG, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Like the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuezhi">Yuezhi</a> fought as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mounted-archer">mounted archers</a>, and the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/records-of-the-grand-historian">Shiji</a></em> credits them with between 100,000 and 200,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a>, a figure that conveys their reputation rather than an exact count. Their wars were dominated by the rise of the Xiongnu. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modu-chanyu">Modu</a> escaped from captivity among them, seized power among the Xiongnu in 209 BCE and turned on the Yuezhi; around 176 BCE a Xiongnu letter to the Han court boasted that they had been crushed. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chanyu">chanyu</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laoshang-chanyu">Laoshang</a> later killed the Yuezhi king and made his skull into a drinking cup. The main body, afterwards called the Greater Yuezhi, then moved west to the Ili valley and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semirechye">Zhetysu</a>, where they defeated the Saka and drove their king south. There they were attacked by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wusun">Wusun</a> under their ruler, the <em>kunmo</em>, whose father had been killed, according to the <em>Han shu</em>, by the Yuezhi. Defeated again, they marched south-west through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferghana-valley">Fergana</a> and, probably in the 130s BCE, crossed into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a>, overwhelming what remained of Greek rule north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindu-kush">Hindu Kush</a>. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhang-qian">Zhang Qian</a> reached them, sent by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-han">Emperor Wu of Han</a> to propose an alliance against the Xiongnu, they had no wish for revenge and declined. Their later wars are almost invisible until the Kushan conquests.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/records-of-the-grand-historian"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/records-of-the-grand-historian.jpg" alt="Records of the Grand Historian" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/records-of-the-grand-historian">Records of the Grand Historian</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%8F%B2%E8%AE%B0.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Zhonghua Book Company, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Little is known of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuezhi">Yuezhi</a> government before their migration beyond the existence of a king, whose death at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> hands broke the confederation. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhang-qian">Zhang Qian</a>, who reached them around 129–128 BCE after ten years as a captive of the Xiongnu, found a ruler set up after the king's death, his heir according to the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/records-of-the-grand-historian">Shiji</a></em> or his widow according to the <em>Han shu</em>, holding court north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxus">Amu Darya</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a> to the south was a tributary land. The <em>Han shu</em> later names a capital, Jianshi. The same history and the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-the-later-han">Hou Han shu</a></em> describe the Yuezhi in Bactria as divided into five <em>xihou</em>, a Chinese rendering of the title <em>yabgu</em>, usually understood as princely chiefs: Xiumi, Shuangmi, Guishuang, Xidun and, in the <em>Hou Han shu</em>, Dumi, where the <em>Han shu</em> has Gaofu. Whether these were Yuezhi clans or Bactrian principalities under Yuezhi overlordship is debated. The arrangement suggests a loose confederation in which a paramount ruler coexisted with powerful regional lords, each controlling towns, valleys and the routes through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamir-mountains">Pamirs</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindu-kush">Hindu Kush</a>. Coins issued in the name of a ruler called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraios">Heraios</a>, probably in the late first century BCE or early first century CE and inscribed in Greek, show that some of these lords presented themselves in Hellenistic style; their relation to the later Kushan kings is uncertain.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/tillya-tepe"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tillya-tepe.jpg" alt="Tillya Tepe" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/tillya-tepe">Tillya Tepe</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tillya_Tepe_crown.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">H Sinica, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuezhi">Yuezhi</a> had an outsized effect on Eurasian history. Their flight from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> set off a chain of migrations that drove the Saka into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and helped end Greek rule in Bactria. The search for them also opened <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> to the West: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhang-qian">Zhang Qian</a>'s mission, though it failed to secure an alliance, gave the Han court its first detailed knowledge of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferghana-valley">Fergana</a>, Sogdiana, Bactria, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthia">Parthia</a> and India, and encouraged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-han">Emperor Wu of Han</a> to push Chinese power into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-regions">Western Regions</a>. It is commonly taken as the beginning of official Chinese contact along the routes that modern writers call the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a>. In Bactria the Yuezhi elite fused steppe traditions with Hellenistic and Iranian urban culture, a synthesis visible at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalchayan">Khalchayan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tillya-tepe">Tillya Tepe</a> and carried forward by their Kushan successors, whose empire sponsored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gandhara-art">Gandharan Buddhist art</a> and helped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> travel to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> and China. If the Yuezhi spoke a Tocharian language, as many scholars believe, they are also connected with the Tocharian Buddhist texts of Kucha and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turfan">Turfan</a>, written between about the sixth and eighth centuries CE. Chinese writers continued to call the Kushans Yuezhi, and the name survived in Chinese records long after the confederation itself had disappeared.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/kujula-kadphises"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kujula-kadphises.jpg" alt="Kujula Kadphises" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/kujula-kadphises">Kujula Kadphises</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coin_of_the_Kushan_king_Kujula_Kadphises.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Classical Numismatic Group;[1], CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuezhi">Yuezhi</a> confederation did not collapse so much as transform. For more than a century after settling in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a> the Greater Yuezhi remained divided among the five <em>xihou</em>, and Chinese sources say little of them. According to the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-the-later-han">Hou Han shu</a></em>, more than a hundred years after the conquest of Bactria the <em>xihou</em> of Guishuang, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kujula-kadphises">Qiujiuque</a>, known from his coins as Kujula Kadphises, attacked and destroyed the other four, made himself king and took the name Guishuang, which Greek and Indian sources render as Kushan. He then invaded Parthian territory, took the region of Gaofu, usually identified with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a>, and conquered Puda and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jibin">Jibin</a> in the borderlands of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. The date of Kujula's unification is uncertain: most scholars place it in the first half of the first century CE, often around 30 CE, and the <em>Hou Han shu</em> says he died at more than eighty years of age. With his rise the Yuezhi state became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushan-empire">Kushan Empire</a>, which under his successors extended deep into northern India. Meanwhile the Lesser Yuezhi, who had stayed behind in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qilian-mountains">Qilian Mountains</a> after the great migration, lived among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qiang-historical-people">Qiang</a> peoples, adopted many of their customs and gradually merged with them during the Han period, so that their separate identity faded.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuezhi">Yuezhi</a>, nomads of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu-corridor">Gansu corridor</a> driven west by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a>, set off migrations that reshaped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a>, drew the Han envoy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhang-qian">Zhang Qian</a> on the journey that opened <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s contacts with the West, and gave rise to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushan-empire">Kushan Empire</a>.</em></p>

